  <title>2.7.3: Sorting Systems of Equations Based on Number of Solutions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00481</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.7.3: Sorting Systems of Equations Based on Number of Solutions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>a63b9950-467a-4851-868e-59795673d248</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>

      <!-- BEGIN CLICK TO REVEAL NO TEXTBOX -->

      <!--Q#1-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Your teacher will give you a <link
              url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a8d7eddfce37fb14146b4b29f9127fc0269ea85f">set
            of cards</link>. Each card contains a system of equations.</para>
            <para>Sort the systems into three
            groups based on the number of solutions each system has. Be prepared to explain how you
            know where each system belongs.</para>

          <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
            compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>

          <list>
            <item>No solution: Cards 2 and 5</item>
            <item>One solution: Cards 1, 4, 6, and 9</item>
            <item>Infinitely many solutions: Cards 3, 7, and 8</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->

      <!-- END CLICK TO REVEAL NO TEXTBOX -->
<section>
  <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
  <section>
    <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
      <!--Ready
      for More Question1-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para>In the cards, for each system with no solution, change a single constant term so
            that there are infinitely many solutions to the system.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>

          <para> Card 2: change 3 to 13; Card 5: change \(-12\) to 12.</para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Ready
      for More Question2-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para>For each system with infinitely many solutions, change a single constant term so
            that there are no solutions to the system.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>

          <para> Any change to a constant term would work.</para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Ready
      for More Question3-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para>Explain why in these situations it is impossible to change a single constant term so
            that there is exactly one solution to the system.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>

          <para> Changing a constant term won’t change the slope of the lines, so the graphs will be
            either distinct parallel lines or the same line.</para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </section>

    <section>
      <note class="self-check">
        <para>Which of the following is true of the following system of
        equations?</para>
  <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;&amp;\left\{\begin{array}{l}7x\;+\;6y\;=\;2\\–28x\;–\;24y\;=\;–8\end{array}\right.\end{array}\)</para>
      </note>
    </section>

    <section>
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section>
        <title>Classifying and Rewriting Systems of Equations</title>
        <para>Find a solution to the system of equations using the addition method.</para>
        <para>\( x + 3y = 2 \) \( 3x + 9y = 6 \)</para>
        <para><emphasis>Solution </emphasis></para>
        <para>With the addition method, we want to eliminate one of the variables by adding the
          equations. In this case, let’s focus on eliminating \( x \). If we multiply both sides of
          the first equation by \( -3 \), then we will be able to eliminate the \( x \)-variable.</para>
        <para>Now add the equations:</para>
        <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl} x + 3y &amp;=&amp; 2 \\ (-3)(x+3y) &amp;=&amp; (-3)(2) \\ -3x -
          9y &amp;=&amp; -6\end{array} \)</para>
        <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl} -3x - 9y &amp;=&amp; -6 \\ + 3x + 9y &amp;=&amp; 6 \\ 0
          &amp;=&amp; 0\end{array} \)</para>
        <para>We can see that there will be an infinite number of situations that satisfy both
          equations.</para>

        <note class="try look-at-me">
        <title>Try It: Classifying and Rewriting Systems of Equations</title>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Change a single constant term in the system from above so that there are no
              solutions.</para>

            <para>  \(\begin{array}{rcl} x + 3y &amp;=&amp; 2 \\ 3x + 9y &amp;=&amp; 6\end{array} \)</para>

            <para>Show that you were successful in changing the system to have no solution.</para>
            
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer: Here is how to rewrite the system so there is no solution.</para>

            <para>We can simply change the 2 in the first equation to any other number, such as 5.</para>

            <para>  \(\begin{array}{rcl} x + 3y &amp;=&amp; 5 \\ 3x + 9y &amp;=&amp; 6\end{array} \)</para>

            <para>Let’s try to prove there is no solution. Using substitution:</para>

            <para>\( \begin{array}{rcl}x + 3y &amp;=&amp; 5 \\ x &amp;=&amp; 5 - 3y\end{array} \)</para>

            <para>Substitute this expression into the second equation.</para>

            <para>\( \begin{array}{rcl}3x + 9y &amp;=&amp; 6 \\ 3(5 - 3y) + 9y &amp;=&amp; 6 \\ 15 -
              9y + 9y &amp;=&amp; 6 \\ 15 &amp;\neq&amp; 6\end{array} \)</para>

            <para>Since the equation is not true, the system has no solution.</para>

          </solution>
        </exercise>
        </note>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
